    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name honors Charles Hackley, a philanthropist who helped build the town’s early lumber mills and later founded a summer resort on the lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_Shores%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1845 settlers organized Norton Township, later becoming part of Muskegon County in 1859 after separating from Ottawa County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norton_Shores%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first sawmill in the area opened in 1847, called Robinson’s Mill, and it sparked a brief boom in lumber production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
